---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import signup from '../../assets/capstone-signup.png';
import inbox from '../../assets/capstone-inbox.png';
import pinned from '../../assets/capstone-pinned.png';
import filters from '../../assets/capstone-filters.png';

const slide = { widths: [640, 1000], sizes: '(min-width: 40rem) 35rem, 92vw' };
---

<Base
  title="Capstone Challenge · David Salazar"
  description="How my Braven team researched why City College students skip campus events, and prototyped changes to the student life app. We won the Fall 2023 Capstone Challenge."
  current="work"
>
  <div class="case">
    <header class="case-head">
      <div class="col">
        <h1 data-reveal>Capstone Challenge</h1>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>
          How can City College get more students to on-campus events after COVID? Our team won the
          Fall 2023 challenge with research and an app prototype.
        </p>
      <div class="brief" data-reveal>
        <p><strong>Problem</strong>City College students weren't going to campus events.</p>
        <p><strong>What I did</strong>Led the user research, wrote the problem statement and prototyped changes to the student life app.</p>
        <p><strong>Result</strong>Our team won the Fall 2023 Capstone Challenge.</p>
      </div>

        <dl class="spec" data-reveal>
          <dt>Program</dt>
          <dd>Braven Accelerator at The City College of New York, fall 2023.</dd>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Team Lead. I shared the manager role with a teammate, led the user research and managed the timeline, and presented the recommendations to academic leadership.</dd>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>Eight students: two managers, two researchers, two prototypers and two deck designers.</dd>
          <dt>Result</dt>
          <dd>Won the Fall 2023 Capstone Challenge. This was a proposal and prototype, and I have no results from the college to report.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>The question</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The challenge was one question: how can City College increase student attendance at
          on-campus events in a post-COVID world? Before suggesting anything, we wanted to know
          who the users were and what they actually experienced. We named two groups, students and
          faculty, and started with empathy research.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>What we heard</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The research turned up two pain points. These are the students' own words from the
          research, shortened.
        </p>
        <div class="runin">
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Too much email, and not the right email.</strong> Students felt they got too
            many messages to keep up with, and the ones they did see weren't about things they cared
            about. One put it this way: “I don't know what events they are holding, when they will
            be held, or where.”
          </p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Not feeling part of the community.</strong> Some students didn't feel they
            belonged, and found it intimidating to go to events alone. Another said the college
            should prioritize better communication channels to build a sense of community.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel" data-reveal>
          <p>
            <strong>Problem statement.</strong> CCNY students don't attend college events because
            they feel disconnected from the school community and believe there are communication
            difficulties between them and the administration.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal>
          We also listed why attendance matters to the school, so the recommendation had a business
          case as well as a user case: community and school spirit, revenue, networking
          opportunities and brand building.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>What we prototyped</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          We started from the college's existing student life app and redesigned the parts the
          research pointed at.
        </p>
        <ul data-reveal>
          <li><strong>A simpler sign-up</strong> with an option to subscribe to the school newsletter, and a warmer home page for newcomers.</li>
          <li><strong>Per-group calendars</strong> in the inbox, so group events stay out of the main calendar and it stops being cluttered.</li>
          <li><strong>Pinned conversations</strong> to keep the important ones at the top.</li>
          <li><strong>Group pages with photos</strong> so groups feel welcoming.</li>
          <li><strong>Better search filters</strong> by school, interests and department, so people can find events that fit them.</li>
          <li><strong>Calendar views</strong> for different preferences.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="slides">
          <figure data-reveal>
            <Image src={signup} alt="Slide showing the current sign-up and feed screens next to the redesigned sign-up and home page" {...slide} />
            <figcaption>Sign-up and home page, current app next to the redesign.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure data-reveal>
            <Image src={inbox} alt="Slide showing the current chat list next to a redesigned inbox with a calendar button for each group" {...slide} />
            <figcaption>Inbox with a calendar for each group.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure data-reveal>
            <Image src={pinned} alt="Slide showing the current chat list next to a redesigned one with pinned conversations at the top" {...slide} />
            <figcaption>Pinned conversations at the top of the chat list.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure data-reveal>
            <Image src={filters} alt="Slide showing the current sort and filter screen next to a redesigned filter by school, interests and department" {...slide} />
            <figcaption>Search filters by school, interests and department.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="figure-note" data-reveal>Slides from our final presentation. The screens are prototypes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>Feedback on the prototype</h2>
        <p data-reveal>We showed the prototype to users and sorted what we heard into what worked and what to grow.</p>
        <div class="runin">
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>What worked.</strong> Pinned conversations, the decluttered calendar, the
            improved search filters and the visuals. People who found signing in difficult said the
            new version simplified it.
          </p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>What to grow.</strong> A way to request to attend an event, class due dates and
            schedules in the same place, videos and stories of what's happening on campus, rating
            surveys at the end of each semester, and a larger number of survey responses.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal>
          One of the “grow” items was a larger number of survey responses, which is a fair limit on how far
          our research goes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>My part</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          I led the user research and turned the pain points into requirements for a more
          accessible app. I worked with academic stakeholders to evaluate options and to shape the
          event-discovery improvements and a go-to-market plan, and I managed the timeline. I
          presented the final recommendations to academic leadership.
        </p>
        <div class="actions" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn" href="/#work">Back to work</a>
          <a class="textlink" href="/#contact">Get in touch</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
